  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Compute Deep Q-Network Bellman targets and mean squared temporal-difference loss with terminal transition masking.\n\nPeriodically freezing target network parameters eliminates feedback loops between value estimates and training targets.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\ny_i = r_i + \\gamma (1 - d_i) \\max_{a'} Q_{\\text{target}}(s_i', a'), \\quad \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{DQN}} = \\frac{1}{B}\\sum (Q(s_i, a_i) - y_i)^2\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Extract Q-values for taken actions from online Q-network\n2. Compute Bellman target using max next-state Q-value from target network\n3. Calculate mean squared TD error loss over the transition batch\n\n### Returns\nScalar float representing DQN MSE loss.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: DQN Loss with Target Network\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNeural networks achieve their remarkable expressive power by stacking linear layers interspersed with non-linear activation functions and normalization layers. Without non-linear activations, any deep network would collapse into a single shallow linear regression. **DQN Loss with Target Network** ensures signals propagate cleanly through dozens or hundreds of layers without vanishing or exploding.\n\nPeriodically freezing target network parameters eliminates feedback loops between value estimates and training targets. In this problem, the goal is to: compute deep q-network bellman targets and mean squared temporal-difference loss with terminal transition masking.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\ny_i = r_i + \\gamma (1 - d_i) \\max_{a'} Q_{\\text{target}}(s_i', a'), \\quad \\mathcal{L}_{\\text{DQN}} = \\frac{1}{B}\\sum (Q(s_i, a_i) - y_i)^2\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Extract Q-values for taken actions from online Q-network\n2. **Phase 2**: Compute Bellman target using max next-state Q-value from target network\n3. **Phase 3**: Calculate mean squared TD error loss over the transition batch\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Gradient Flow**: Carefully designed activation functions (like ReLU, GELU, and Swish) maintain strong gradient signals during backpropagation.\n* **Internal Covariate Shift**: Normalization techniques (like LayerNorm and RMSNorm) standardize hidden states across channels or batch dimensions, allowing models to train stably at higher learning rates.\n* **Hardware Friendly**: Modern deep learning frameworks implement these layers using fused GPU kernels to reduce memory round-trips.",
  "examples": [
